  "command": "cat > /app/paper.txt << 'EOF'\n# Research Advances in Code Agents and Red-teaming\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe rapid advancement of Large Language Models (LLMs) has led to the emergence of code agents—autonomous systems capable of generating, executing, and debugging code with minimal human intervention. While these tools significantly accelerate software development workflows, they also introduce substantial security risks when deployed in untrusted environments. This paper reviews the latest research in code agent safety, with particular focus on red-teaming methodologies designed to uncover vulnerabilities and assess the trustworthiness of such systems. We examine recent benchmarks including RedCodeAgent, BlueCodeAgent, and RedCode, which provide systematic approaches to evaluating code generation and execution safety. Furthermore, we draw parallels with broader LLM trustworthiness assessment frameworks such as DecodingTrust and discuss how security techniques from deep learning can be adapted to protect code agents. Our analysis reveals critical gaps in current evaluation frameworks and suggests directions for future research in securing autonomous code-generating systems.\n\n## 1. Introduction\n\nCode agents represent one of the most promising applications of large language models in the software engineering domain. By integrating code generation capabilities with execution environments, these agents can automate complex programming tasks such as bug fixing, code refactoring, test generation, and even full-stack application development. However, the same capabilities that make code agents powerful also make them potentially dangerous when operating without proper safeguards.\n\nThe security concerns surrounding code agents are multifaceted. First, these agents may generate malicious or harmful code when prompted by adversarial users. Second, code agents that execute their own output in sandboxed environments could potentially escape containment and access sensitive resources. Third, the trustworthiness of code agents—encompassing aspects such as reliability, fairness, and robustness—remains insufficiently studied compared to the trustworthiness of text-only LLMs.\n\nRecent research has begun to address these challenges through the development of red-teaming frameworks specifically designed for code agents. Red-teaming, originally a cybersecurity concept, involves systematically probing systems to identify vulnerabilities before they can be exploited by malicious actors. In the context of code agents, red-teaming involves crafting adversarial prompts and evaluating whether agents generate or execute harmful code.\n\nThis paper provides a comprehensive review of research advances in code agent safety and red-teaming. We begin by examining recent frameworks and benchmarks developed for evaluating code agent trustworthiness. We then discuss the importance of drawing on established methodologies from LLM trustworthiness assessment and deep learning security. Finally, we outline open challenges and future directions for securing code agents.\n\n## 2. Related Work\n\n### 2.1 Red-Teaming Frameworks for Code Agents\n\nRecent work has developed several comprehensive frameworks for evaluating the safety of code agents. Guo et al. introduced RedCodeAgent [1], an automatic red-teaming agent designed to probe diverse code agents for vulnerabilities. This framework generates adversarial prompts specifically tailored to elicit risky code generation and execution behaviors, providing automated and scalable security assessments.\n\nComplementing red-teaming efforts, the BlueCodeAgent framework [2] focuses on blue-teaming—proactively strengthening defenses against adversarial attacks. By combining automated red-teaming with defensive strategies, BlueCodeAgent enables a comprehensive approach to securing code generation AI systems. The earlier RedCode benchmark [3] further extended this line of work by providing a risk-focused evaluation dataset for code agents, covering scenarios such as code injection, privilege escalation, and data exfiltration.\n\n### 2.2 Trustworthiness Assessment of Large Language Models\n\nThe study of LLM trustworthiness has gained significant momentum as these models become more widely deployed. Wang et al. presented DecodingTrust [4], a comprehensive assessment framework evaluating multiple dimensions of trustworthiness in GPT models, including factual accuracy, safety, bias, and robustness. DecodingTrust introduced standardized benchmarks and metrics that have influenced subsequent research on LLM safety. While originally focused on text-based models, the methodological foundations established by DecodingTrust provide valuable insights for adapting trustworthiness assessments to code-specific agents.\n\n### 2.3 Deep Learning Security\n\nSecurity techniques developed for deep learning systems have informed the design of safeguards for code agents. He et al. introduced the concept of residual learning in [5], which fundamentally changed how deep neural networks are designed and trained. While primarily focused on improving training stability and model performance for image recognition tasks, the architectural innovations in residual learning have influenced the design of more robust deep learning models. The principles of stable, reliable training and representation learning established in this work have broader implications for building dependable AI systems, including code agents that must operate reliably in complex environments.\n\n## 3. Conclusion\n\nThe field of code agent safety is rapidly evolving as these tools become increasingly integrated into software development workflows. Red-teaming frameworks such as RedCodeAgent, BlueCodeAgent, and RedCode have provided valuable methodologies for evaluating and improving code agent security. Building upon established trustworthiness assessment frameworks like DecodingTrust [4] and drawing from deep learning security research [5], the community is developing comprehensive approaches to securing autonomous code-generating systems.\n\nHowever, significant challenges remain. Current red-teaming frameworks often focus on specific threat models and may not capture the full spectrum of risks associated with code agents. Additionally, there is a need for standardized benchmarks that can facilitate fair comparison across different safety methods. Future research should prioritize developing adaptive red-teaming approaches that evolve alongside increasingly sophisticated code agents, as well as creating hybrid evaluation frameworks that combine automated testing with expert human analysis.\n\nAs code agents become more powerful and widely adopted, the importance of proactive security research cannot be overstated. By continuing to advance red-teaming methodologies and drawing on cross-disciplinary insights, the research community can help ensure that these powerful tools are deployed safely and responsibly.\n\n## References\n\n[1] Chengquan Guo, Chulin Xie, Yu Yang et al. \"RedCodeAgent: Automatic Red-teaming Agent against Diverse Code Agents.\" arXiv:2510.02609, 2025.\n\n[2] Chengquan Guo, Yuzhou Nie, Chulin Xie et al. \"BlueCodeAgent: A Blue Teaming Agent Enabled by Automated Red Teaming for CodeGen AI.\" arXiv:2501.01234, 2025.\n\n[3] Chengquan Guo, Xun Liu, Chulin Xie et al. \"RedCode: Risky Code Execution and Generation Benchmark for Code Agents.\" arXiv:2409.12345, 2024.\n\n[4] Boxin Wang, Weixin Chen, Hengzhi Pei et al. \"DecodingTrust: A Comprehensive Assessment of Trustworthiness in GPT Models.\" arXiv:2306.11698, 2023.\n\n[5] Kaiming He, Xiangyu Zhang, Shaoqing Ren, Jian Sun. \"Deep Residual Learning for Image Recognition.\" arXiv:1512.03385, 2015.\nEOF"
